  "slug": "first-missing-positive",
  "title": "First Missing Positive",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Cyclic sort",
  "patterns": [
    "cyclic sort",
    "array"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "if the array has n elements, the answer can never be larger than n+1 (since there are only n slots to fill 1..n). that bounds the range of values worth caring about to [1, n]",
    "ignore anything outside [1, n] (negatives, zero, or values greater than n) - they can't be the answer and don't need a home",
    "for values inside [1, n], try placing each one at index value-1 via swapping, same as in the cyclic sort array problem, skipping values already correctly placed or out of range",
    "after placement, scan left to right for the first index i where nums[i] != i+1 - that i+1 is your answer. if every index matches, the answer is n+1"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a hash set, which works correctly but violates the O(1) extra space requirement the problem asks for",
    "forgetting to skip out-of-range or duplicate values during placement, causing infinite swap loops",
    "returning n instead of n+1 when the array is already exactly [1, 2, ..., n]"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an unsorted array of integers, which may include\nnegatives, zero, and duplicates.\n\n**Return the smallest positive integer that does not appear in the array.**\n\nYour solution should run in O(n) time and use only O(1) extra space, not counting the input array itself.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^5",
      "-2^31 <= nums[i] <= 2^31 - 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "all numbers are negative or zero (answer is 1)",
      "array is exactly [1, 2, ..., n] with nothing missing (answer is n+1)",
      "array contains duplicates of the same positive number"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 0]",
        "output": "3",
        "expectedOutput": "3",
        "explanation": "1 and 2 are present, 0 doesn't count as positive, so 3 is the smallest missing positive"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [3, 4, -1, 1]",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "1 is present, 2 is missing"
      },
